  "query_preview": "..\nThe low pressure system that is keeping Southeast Arizona in \nthe 80s is currently to the north of the area in the Great \nbasin. Outside of temperatures, the main impact with this system\nis the breezy winds. As of 1 pm, the highest wind gusts have \nbeen mostly 25-35 mph in the eastern valleys, with a recorded \ngust of 44 mph in Willcox. These winds will begin to decrease \nand taper off at or around sunset. As this system begins to exit\nthe region to the northeast of the area tomorrow, the area will\nremain breezy, however will be less than today. This system \nwill keep high temperatures 'cooler' tomorrow and below normal \nin the mid 80s to low 90s. \n\nOnce this system passes, a ridge begins to build over Texas,\nintroducing southwest to southerly flow, allowing for \ntemperatures begin to increase Sunday into next week. This \nincrease brings back the chances for temperatures in the 100s \nearly to mid next week, with current NBM probabilities of Tucson\nreaching 100 degrees at 25% Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday.\nDepending on how the high pressure sets up, determining the\noverall upper level flow, some moisture may be advected and\nmoved into the region mid next week. As of now, much of this\nmoisture is in the eastern valleys, introducing afternoon and\nevening shower and thunderstorm chances (15-50%), with the \nhighest chances in the White Mountains and Sky Islands.",
